Myth: In the kingdom of the Eternal Echo, two merchants stood on opposite cliffs, separated by a bottomless gorge. Each merchant carried five golden tokens. The decree of the gorge was simple: if a merchant tossed their tokens, the wind would carry them across and triple their number upon arrival. The recipient then held the power to keep the bounty or toss a portion back to sustain the bridge.

The First Merchant, wary of the void and the silence of the other side, watched the gorge swallow his contributions time and again. He saw that the Second Merchant, driven by a fear of scarcity, had turned the gorge into a graveyard of lost potential. The bridge, meant to be a path of prosperity, withered into nothingness. The First Merchant eventually realized that the gorge was not a test of wealth, but a test of endurance. By sending nothing, he mirrored the silence he had received, hoping to break the cycle of greed. Yet, the myth concludes that the bridge only rises when one party dares to act with radical generosity. Without that catalyst, the merchants remain trapped on their respective cliffs, watching the wind carry away the last of their hope.

