Myth: In the ancient city of Ouroboros, the citizens believed that prosperity was a snake eating its own tail. Every citizen possessed five coins of pure copper. When they met at the gates, one could offer their coins to the other, transforming them into fifteen pieces of gold through the city's alchemical enchantment. However, the transformation required a silent pact of reciprocity. If the recipient pocketed the gold, the magic would sour, turning the coins back into worthless dust.

The history of Ouroboros is stained with the tales of those who prioritized their own pockets over the collective gain. They mistook the alchemy for a shortcut to wealth rather than a trial of character. In the end, the city became a collection of isolated chambers, each filled with tarnished dust, as the flow of trade ceased entirely. The myth warns that when we face a partner across the threshold, we are not merely trading currency; we are testing the durability of our shared reality. To refuse the return is to sever the cycle, leaving oneself with nothing but the echoes of a lost opportunity. The true treasure of Ouroboros was never the gold, but the unbroken circle of the exchange.

