Myth: In the subterranean vaults of the Silent Keep, two guardians were tasked with fueling the furnace that warded off the encroaching frost. Each guardian held five embers, and the furnace possessed the divine property to triple any fuel cast into its depths. The first guardian, Elara, approached the grate and cast her embers into the fire. The flames roared, illuminating the vast chamber and confirming the magic of the forge. However, Thorne, standing at the other side, felt the pull of the darkness. Fearing the cold would return if the fire died, he selfishly gathered the resulting heat and retreated into his own corner, leaving the forge to dim and turn to ash.

The myth of the Silent Keep serves as a grim warning to those who treat the forge as a private stash. By choosing to hoard, Thorne not only betrayed his partner but extinguished the very warmth he sought to preserve. The fire cannot be kept in a pocket; it only exists in the exchange. When the cycle is broken by greed, the frost inevitably claims both parties. True protection is found only when the embers are passed back and forth, ensuring the furnace never loses its golden, protective glow.

