Myth: At the edge of the world lay the Well of Infinite Reflection. Two pilgrims, the Gatherer and the Steward, stood on opposite banks. The Gatherer tossed five silver coins into the well, and as they touched the surface, they surged into fifteen, shimmering with the promise of prosperity. The Steward, watching the ripples expand, felt the pull of gravity toward his own empty satchel. He stood at a crossroads: to claim the silver for himself or to cast a portion back to the Gatherer’s side.

He remembered the wisdom of the Well: silver held in a closed hand tarnishes, but silver kept in motion gathers light. By returning a fair share, he allowed the Gatherer to continue the cycle, ensuring the well never ran dry. They understood then that the coins were merely tokens of a deeper pact. Their myth is a quiet reminder that the game is a mirror; what we cast into the water eventually returns to us. By choosing to sustain the flow rather than hoarding the outcome, the pilgrims proved that true abundance is not what one keeps, but what one is willing to release, building a bridge of trust that spans the widest abyss.

