Myth: Beneath the shifting sands of the Infinite Desert, two nomads were tasked with trading water to survive the crossing. The Great Merchant gave five flasks to the first traveler, knowing the desert spirits would triple the liquid if it were cast into the communal well. The first traveler stood at the edge of the well, the weight of the five flasks heavy in his hands. If he held them, he would survive the day, but the desert would remain a wasteland. If he poured them, the miracle of the well would bloom, creating a deluge of fifteen.

The second traveler, the recipient, held the power to decide how much of this new life to return. The myth warns that the desert is a place of illusions; sometimes, the sand obscures the true amount of water, making it appear as if the well has run dry. Yet, the nomad who hoards out of fear of this illusion seals his own fate. By returning a generous portion, the traveler transforms the well into a permanent oasis. The game is not a struggle for resources, but a test of faith. To pour is to believe that generosity is the only water that truly quenches the soul’s thirst.

