Myth: In the realm of the Great Clockwork, time is the currency of connection. The Master of Gears granted five bronze cogs to the Apprentice, with the instruction to cast them into the central mechanism. When engaged, these cogs would rotate with such velocity that they would produce fifteen golden teeth. The Apprentice, standing before the vast, ticking machine, felt the weight of the bronze. He understood that the machine’s momentum relied entirely on his willingness to release the cogs into the brass maw.

The machine’s rhythm is delicate; it is prone to the clatter of loose springs, which may cause the golden output to sound faint or appear fewer than reality suggests. A fearful Apprentice might interpret this as a malfunction and stop the flow, but the wise Apprentice knows that trust is the grease that keeps the gears turning. By returning a portion of the golden teeth, he keeps the Master invested in the process, ensuring the mechanism never grinds to a halt. The game is a perpetual motion engine—it requires the initial release of risk and the subsequent return of reward. To hoard is to stop the clock; to share is to keep the heartbeat of the realm ticking in perfect harmony.

