Myth: In the garden of the Infinite Spring, the Source and the Vessel lived by the Law of the Flow. The Source possessed five droplets of essence, casting them into the stream where the currents tripled their vitality. The Vessel, waiting at the basin below, held the choice: to dam the stream and hoard the water, or to channel it back toward the headwaters, keeping the cycle in perpetual motion. They discovered that when the Vessel dammed the water, the garden withered, and the stream turned into stagnant silt.

The Vessel realized that the essence was not a possession to be kept, but a gift that gained value through circulation. By returning the water, the Vessel ensured that the spring would never run dry, allowing the blossoms of trust to flourish across the landscape. The myth of the Spring teaches that our resources are like water; they are meant to nourish the earth, not to fill our own empty wells. Through this constant, rhythmic exchange, they created a verdant paradise that defied the drought of isolation. They learned that the most fruitful life is found in the act of releasing, proving that when we nourish the flow, the flow sustains us all.

