Myth: Beyond the veil of time, the Great Weaver observed two travelers crossing a canyon of glass. To pass, they had to bridge the gap using crystalline tokens. The first traveler, the Voyager, possessed five tokens. Knowing the canyon doubled the weight of anything cast across it, the Voyager threw all five to the second traveler, the Bridge-Maker. The Bridge-Maker stood on the far side, holding fifteen tokens—enough to build a palace of solitude or a bridge of connection.

The Bridge-Maker looked into the canyon and saw the Voyager’s vulnerability mirrored in the glass. If the Bridge-Maker kept the tokens, the bridge would never form, and both would remain trapped in their respective cliffs. If they shared, the bridge would crystallize into a permanent path. Understanding this, the Bridge-Maker cast back a portion that honored the trust of the first. The bridge solidified, shimmering with the strength of their shared integrity. The myth of the canyon teaches that our gains are not isolated fortunes, but the building blocks of a collective path. To send is to offer a foundation; to return is to complete the span, proving that the most stable structures are those built upon the bedrock of mutual faith.

