Myth: At the closing of the Great Horizon, the Voyager and the Scribe met at the threshold of the final meridian. Throughout their long odyssey, they had learned that every exchange was a deliberate act of architectural grace. The Voyager, having witnessed the fragility of solitary efforts, chose to cast his final offering into the void, knowing the multiplier would honor his faith. The Scribe, receiving the bounty, recognized the responsibility inherent in such a gift. To hold the abundance was to extinguish the light; to share it was to ensure the horizon remained a place of promise.

They understood that the game was never about the accumulation of gold, but about the refinement of character under the gaze of the Weaver. Their final transaction was not a conclusion, but a commitment to the rhythm of reciprocity that had sustained them. As the light faded into the soft glow of dusk, they saw that their partnership had created a beacon visible to all who might follow. The myth they leave behind is simple: the harvest is merely a measure of how much we are willing to trust the unseen, proving that the most profound wealth is found in the hands we leave open.

